OVERVIEW:

This software facilitates programming on MGTP(Model Generation Theorem
Prover)[1] which is a bottom-up theorem proving system running on
KLIC. Many kind of functions are available through options.

This software contains the following tools(command names are
surrounded by parenthesis):

(1) MGTP Translator(mg2kl1): compiles MG clauses to KL1 clauses which
is linked with MGTP engine so as to determine satisfiability of MG
clauses.

(2) MGTP Interpreter(intMerc): is an MGTP inference engine which can
interpret MG clauses directly, and so MGTP Translator is not
necessary.

(3) Helpful Commands:
   (a) Range-restricted Checker(rchk): judges range-restrictedness of
       the set of MG clauses.
   (b) DOM Inserter(dom): inserts dom predicates into the antecedent of
       non-range-restricted MG clauses and adds MG clauses which define 
       the dom predicate.
   (c) MG-clause Counter(mc): displays a count of positive MG clauses,
       mixed MG clauses, negative MG clauses and other type terms 
       in a file.
   (d) MERC-pattern Generator(mercpat): analyze the antecedent of a MG clause 
       and produce MERC-pattern for conjunctive matching of MGTP engine.
   (e) MG-clauses Redundancy Eliminator(simplify): deletes MG clauses which
      represent tautology and remove redundant literals of MG clauses 
      in order to decrease proof time.
   (f) TPTP-to-MGTP Translator(tptp2mgtp): translates TPTP[3]-style
       problem into MG clauses.

(4) Pre-processors for pruning search space: 

   (a) FD(Folding Down)[2] Translator(fdown): translates a MG clause into
       another MG clause containing negated atom to incorporate FD in
       MGTP proving. Translated MG clauses have to linked with 
       Constraints MGTP engine.

   (b) NHM(Non Horn Magic Set) Translator(mg2dnhm): translates a MG
       clause into other MG clauses to incorporate NHM in MGTP proving.

(5) MGTP Tracer: traces proving process in MGTP 

(6) An Interactive Programming Environment for KLIC(klic_run)


INSTALLATION:
All tools are totally written in KL1 and can be compiled using KLIC.
This distribution includes 'src' and 'bin' directories.
The 'src' contains all KL1 sources for the tools and the 'bin' contains 
mg2dnhm and klic_run defined as shellscripts.

The following items indicate relations between commands and source files.

   (1) mg2kl1: mg2kl1.kl1
   (2) intMerc: intMerc.kl1, match.kl1
   (3) dom: dom.kl1
   (4) mc: mc.kl1
   (5) mercpat: mercpat.kl1, unify.kl1
   (6) rchk: rangeCheck.kl1
   (7) simplify: simplify.kl1
   (8) tptp2mgtp: tptp2mgtp.kl1
   (9) fdown: folddown.kl1
  (10) mg2dnhm: nhmmode and nhmtrans installations are necessary
  (11) nhmmode: nhmmode.kl1
  (12) nhmtrans: nhmtrans.kl1
  (13) kl1mod: kl1mod.kl1, fm.kl1

Typing in
klic -o 'command name' 'source files(*.kl1)'
produces the command 'command name' which works as shell command.

REFERENCES:

[1] H. Fujita and R. Hasegawa: A Model Generation Theorem Prover in
KL1 Using a Ramified-Stack Algorithm, Proc. 8th ICLP, pp.535-548 (1991)

[2] R. Letz, K. Mayr and C. Goller: Controlled Intergration of the Cut
Rule into Connection Tableau Calculi, J. of Automated Reasoning,
Vol.13, No. 3, pp.297-337 (1994)

[3] C. Suttner, G. Sutcliffe and T. Yemenis: The TPTP Problem Library,
Proc. 12th CADE, pp.252-266 (1993)
